<h1>shuffle sort</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="distributionSort.html"><em>distribution sort</em></a> algorithm that begins by removing the first 1/8 of the n items, sorting them (recursively), and putting them in an array.  This creates n/8 <a href="bucket.html"><em>buckets</em></a> to which the remaining 7/8 of the items are distributed.  Each bucket is then sorted, and the buckets are concatenated.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="distributionSort.html"><em>distribution sort</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="j_sort.html"><em>J sort</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="americanFlagSort.html"><em>American flag sort</em></a>, <a href="distributivePartitioningSort.html"><em>distributive partitioning sort</em></a>, <a href="btree.html"><em>B-tree</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Shuffle sort can be thought of as forming very wide <a href="tree.html"><em>trees</em></a>, like B-tree with m=n/8, to sort efficiently in many cases. </p> 
<p> Shuffle sort estimates the distribution of the items to be sorted by examining the first n/8 items.  Distributive partitioning sort estimates the distribution by approximating the median and linearly interpolating from the minimum to the median and from the median to the maximum.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
Explained in <a href="https://groups.google.com/g/fido7.ru.algorithms/c/dL4SAH96OPY/m/s4oABNtMCCYJ" target="_blank">a message about J sort</a> posted in 1997.
</p>

